AVM: usb-camera, a USB Video Class webcam fed by the Mac's camera

QEMU has no camera device, and a Mac's built-in camera isn't a USB device
that usb-host or usb-redir could pass through (2026-10-27). usb-camera is
a UVC 1.1 camera, which Windows and Linux drive with their own drivers:
YUY2 at 1280x720 or 640x480, 30 frames a second, on a high-speed bulk
endpoint (one alternate setting, nothing isochronous to negotiate). Its
pictures come from the Mac's default camera through AVFoundation, scaled
to the committed size.

The Mac's camera opens when the guest commits a format, and closes on a
CLEAR_FEATURE(ENDPOINT_HALT) of the video endpoint (how Linux stops a bulk
stream) or when a finished frame has gone unread for 3 seconds (Windows
cancels its transfers), so macOS's camera light is on only while
something in the guest uses it. It says so on stderr ("usb-camera: ...
is on", "... is off", access not allowed), which AVM speaks. The device
is migratable, so a machine with it can still be saved; the camera
reopens at the guest's next read after a restore.

--- a/hw/usb/meson.build
+++ b/hw/usb/meson.build
@@ -47,6 +47,16 @@
   system_ss.add(when: 'CONFIG_USB_STORAGE_MTP', if_true: files('dev-mtp.c'))
 endif
 
